uth-string <string> | --p8-file-path <path>]\n                  [--keychain <filename> | --sync]\n\n\nExit codes: 0 success, 1 failure (Upon failure, an error code and message are generally also displayed.)\n\nAuthentication: Most commands require authorization.\n                There are two methods available: user name with password, and apiKey with apiIssuer.\n\n            -u, --username <username>     Username. Required to connect for validation and upload.\n            -p, --password <password>     Password. Required if username specified. If this argument is not supplied on the command line,\n                                          it will be read from stdin.\n                                          Alternatively to entering <password> in plaintext, it may also be specified using a '@keychain:'\n                                          or '@env:' prefix followed by a keychain password item name or environment variable name.\n                                          Example: '-p @keychain:<name>' uses the password stored in the keychain password item named <name>.\n                                                                         You can create and update keychain items with the\n                                                                         --store-password-in-keychain-item command. Note also that the\n                                                                         --username can be inferred from the keychain item so --username\n                                                                         can be omitted when using a '-p @keychain:' option.\n                                          Example: '-p @env:<variable>'  uses the value in the environment variable named <variable>\n            --api-key <api_key>           ApiKey. Required for JWT authentication (in lieu of username/password).\n                                          This option will search the following directories in sequence for a private key file\n                                          with the name of 'AuthKey_<api_key>.p8': './private_keys', '~/private_keys', '~/.private_keys',\n                                          and '~/.appstoreconnect/private_keys'. Additionally, you can set environment variable $API_PRIVATE_KEYS_DIR\n                                          or a user default API_PRIVATE_KEYS_DIR to specify the directory where your AuthKey file is located.\n                                          Individual JWT keys are in the form 'ApiKey_<api_key>.p8'.\n            --api-issuer <issuer_id>      Issuer ID. Required if --apiKey is specified.\n--\n     --upload-package <file>              Uploads the specified app archive to the App Store. Authentication is required.\n            -t, --platform {macos | ios | appletvos | visionos} Specify the platform of the file.\n            --apple-id <id>               Specifies the Apple ID of the app package.\n            --bundle-version <str>        Specifies the CFBundleVersion of the app package.\n            --bundle-short-version-string <str> Specifies the CFBundleShortVersionString of the app package.\n            --provider-public-id <id>     Required with --upload-package when a user account is associated with multiple providers and using\n                                          username/password authentication. You can use the --list-providers command to retrieve the providers\n                                          associated with your account. Alternatively, you may use --team-id or --asc-public-id.\n            --wait                        Waits until the status is done PROCESSING to return the build-status.\n     --upload-app -f <filepath>            Uploads the specified app to the App Store. Authentication is required.\n\n     --validate-app <file>                Validates an app archive for the App Store. Authentication is required.\n            -f, --file <file>             <file> specifies the path to the file to process.\n\n     --list-apps                          Display all apps associated with a provider.\n            --provider-public-id <id>     Specifies the publid id of the provider whose apps should be listed.\n            --filter-apple-id <id>        Filter the list of apps by Apple ID.\n            --filter-bundle-id <id>       Filter the list of apps by bundleID.\n            --filter-name <name>          Filter the list of apps by name.\n            --filter-sku <SKU>            Filter the list of apps by SKU.\n            --filter-platform <string>    Filter the list of apps by platform {macos | ios | appletvos | visionos}.\n\n     --list-providers                     Displays a list of the providers and their attributes associated with your account.\n                                          This command is useful to determine what to use with the --provider-public-id and --team-id options.\n                                          Authentication is required.\n            --legacy                      If specified, outputs provider information in a format that older versions of altool used.\n\n     --store-password-in-keychain-item <name_for_keychain_item> -u <username> -p <password>\n                                          Stores the password <password> in the keychain item named <name_for_keychain_item> associated with the account <username>.\n                                          If an item with that name and account already exists in the keychain, its password will be updated. Otherwise a new item\n                                          is created with that name. You can use this keychain item with the -p option to mask your password with other commands.\n                                          Example: altool --store-password-in-keychain-item MY_SECRET -u jappleseed@apple.com -p \"MyP@ssw0rd!@78\"\n            --keychain <filename>         Used with --store-password-in-keychain and the @keychain: option for -p lets you specify the path to a keychain\n                                          file in which to store or from which to read the password. Cannot be used with the --sync option.\n            --sync                        Used with --store-password-in-keychain to allow the keychain item to sync with your iCloud account and other devices\n                                          associated with the account. Cannot by used with the --keychain option.\n\n     --app-store-text <folder_path>       Allows you to download or upload the App Store Texts. The files can be edited to update existing,\n                                          or new files can be added for new locals and then re-uploaded to the App Store. More detailed instructions provided in\n                                          README.md which is in the download folder from a --download operation.\n                                          The App Store Texts are comprised of description, keywords, promotionalText, whatsNew, supportUrl and marketingUrl. (macOS 13.0 or later)\n            --upload                      Specify to upload the changes from the designated folder.\n            --download                    Specify to download the existing localization values in the designated folder.\n            --apple-id <id>               Specifies the Apple ID of the app package.\n            --bundle-short-version-string <str>  Specifies the CFBundleShortVersionString of the app package.\n--\n     --upload-asset-pack <filepath>       Uploads the specified asset pack to the App Store. Authentication is required.\n                                          filepath must be specified asset pack (.aar file).\n            --apple-id <id>               Specifies the Apple ID of the app to which the asset pack will be associated.\n            --wait                        Waits until the status is READY, FAILED or NULL to return the status.\n\n     --asset-pack-status                  Return the status of the asset pack specified by either the --asset-pack-vers